<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A tribute to Ada Lovelace and her vision for computing."
    />
    <title>Ada Lovelace | A Tribute</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <main id="main">
      <header class="page-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">A tribute to imagination and invention</p>
        <h1 id="title">Ada Lovelace</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">
          She saw possibilities in a machine that existed mostly on paper.
        </p>
      </header>

      <figure id="img-div">
        <img
          id="image"
          src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ada_Lovelace_portrait.jpg?width=700"
          alt="Watercolor portrait of Ada Lovelace wearing a dark dress"
        />
        <figcaption id="img-caption">
          A watercolor portrait of Ada Lovelace by Alfred Edward Chalon.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section id="tribute-info" aria-labelledby="story-title">
        <h2 id="story-title">More than a calculation</h2>

        <p>
          Ada Lovelace was an English mathematician who worked with
          Charles Babbage on ideas surrounding his proposed Analytical
          Engine. In 1843, she translated an article about the machine
          and added extensive notes of her own.
        </p>

        <p>
          Her notes described a sequence of operations for calculating
          Bernoulli numbers. They also explored a bigger idea: a machine
          that manipulates symbols might do more than arithmetic. That
          imagination is why her work remains an important part of
          computing history.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “The Analytical Engine weaves algebraic patterns, just as
            the Jacquard-loom weaves flowers and leaves.”
          </p>
          <cite>— Ada Lovelace</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="learn-more">
          Read more about Ada Lovelace on
          <a
            id="tribute-link"
            href="https://www.britannica.com/biography/Ada-Lovelace"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Britannica</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
